          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            On the very largest scales observable in astrophysical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRW+model"} FRW model
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            (but see at
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhomogeneous+cosmology"} inhomogeneous cosmology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            and with primordial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+inflation"} cosmic inflation
            (called the
            = succeed "-CDM" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
            = succeed "\u201D)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} concordance model of cosmology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horizon"} horizon
            in there and everything on smaller scales is the
            = succeed "." do
              %em observable universe
          %p
            There has never been a reason to assume that beyond this
            %em cosmic horizon
            visible to us today, the cosmos would not extend further. In fact in the simple
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRW+models"} FRW models
            with positive cosmological constant, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            is not a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+manifold"} closed manifold
            and extends indefinitely beyond our observable horizon. This is directly analogous to the ancient mariner who would stand on the deck of his ship and see a few miles of ocean around him. That was the world within his horizon, but there was no telling what lay beyond.
          %p
            Therefore it is important to distinguish the
            %em observable universe
            from the
            %em universe
            as such. Unfortunately, intellectual laziness tended to ignore this distinction and at some point some people who said “universe” to mean just the stretch of our cosmic horizon felt the need to have a new term for whatever may lie beyond. For better or worse, that new term has become wide-spread these days and is “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiverse"}> multiverse
            ”.
          %p
            The main scientific observation that goes with this is the observation that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+inflation"} cosmic inflation
            – for which there is by now excellent and ever increasing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experimental
            evidence that indeed it happened – does, at least by the simple method which is currently used to model it, naturally predict a large ambient space in which local regions stochastically undergo inflationary expansion. This scenario of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chaotic+inflation"} chaotic inflation
            has in fact been singled out as the most likely one (within the standard model) by the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicep2"} bicep2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            in 2014.
          %p
            In any case, as far as the
            %em observable
            universe is concerned, two facts are worth noticing:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the observable universe alone is already pretty darn large, see the various discussion of its scales
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "#References"} below
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giordano+Bruno"}> Giordano Bruno
                ‘s old intuition remains plausible even at the largest scales: possibly the full xyz-verse beyond our present horizon is vastly larger still… and we may never know.
          %div{:style => "float:left;margin:0 10px 10px 0;"}
            %img{:src => "http://www.learner.org/courses/physics/visual/img_half/length_scale.jpg", :width => "220"}/
          %p
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/CosmicOuroboros.jpg", :width => "440"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics taken from
              %a{:href => "#PrimackCh6"} Primack Chapter 6
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "http://viewfromthecenter.com/images/Cosmic_Uroboros.jpg"}> color version
              )
          %blockquote
            %p Recent measurements (c. 2001) by a number of ground-based and balloon-based experiments, including MAT/TOCO, Boomerang, Maxima, and DASI, have shown that the brightest spots are about 1 degree across. Thus the universe was known to be flat to within about 15% accuracy prior to the WMAP results. WMAP has confirmed this result with very high accuracy and precision. We now know that the universe is flat with only a 2% margin of error
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/energy"} energy
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/length+scales"} length scales
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            (from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmic
            scales, over
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+particle"} fundamental particle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/masses"} masses
            around the
